# 🐧 Basic Linux Commands Cheat Sheet

A beginner-friendly guide to essential Linux terminal commands for system navigation, file management, and system information.

---

## 📂 File & Directory Navigation
Manage and traverse directories within the terminal.

*   **`pwd`** — Print Working Directory. Displays the full path of your current location.
*   **`ls`** — List files and folders in the current directory.
    *   `ls -l` — Long format listing (shows permissions, owner, size, and date).
    *   `ls -a` — Lists all files, including hidden ones (files starting with a `.`).
*   **`cd [directory]`** — Change Directory to move to a specific folder.
    *   `cd ..` — Move up one level to the parent directory.
    *   `cd ~` — Go directly to your home directory.

## 📄 File & Directory Operations
Create, copy, move, and delete files or folders.

*   **`mkdir [folder_name]`** — Create a new directory.
*   **`touch [file_name]`** — Create an empty file.
*   **`cp [source] [destination]`** — Copy files.
    *   `cp -r [source_dir] [dest_dir]` — Copy a directory and all its contents recursively.
*   **`mv [source] [destination]`** — Move or rename files and directories.
*   **`rm [file_name]`** — Delete a file.
*   **`rm -r [folder_name]`** — Delete a folder and all its contents permanently.

## 🔍 Viewing & Editing Files
Read and inspect file data directly from the terminal.

*   **`cat [file_name]`** — Display the entire content of a file.
*   **`head -n [number] [file_name]`** — View the first *n* lines of a file.
*   **`tail -n [number] [file_name]`** — View the last *n* lines of a file.
*   **`less [file_name]`** — Open a file in a scrollable view (press `q` to exit).
*   **`grep "[search_term]" [file_name]`** — Search for a specific word or phrase inside a file.

## 🛠️ System Information & Permissions
Check system status and modify access controls.

*   **`uname -a`** — Display detailed system and kernel information.
*   **`df -h`** — Show available disk space in human-readable format.
*   **`top`** — Display real-time running processes and system resource usage (press `q` to exit).
*   **`chmod [permissions] [file_name]`** — Change file read/write/execute permissions (e.g., `chmod +x script.sh`).
*   **`sudo [command]`** — Execute a command with administrative/root privileges.
*   **`clear`** — Clear the terminal screen.
